j-raw>=0D=0A                            <h2 class=3D"corner-header"> <img s=
rc=3D"https://assets.codepen.io/t-1/chriscorner.png" style=3D"width:=0D=0A =
           65px; border-radius: 4px" /> Chris=E2=80=99 Corner </h2>=0D=0A  =
                          <p class=3D"corner-intro"> A collection of web de=
sign and development news and thoughts from CodePen's own Chris Coyier. </p=
>=0D=0A                          </mj-raw>=0D=0A                          <=
p>Gradients can look <em>super</em> different depending on what color space=
 you have them interpolate in. It=E2=80=99s sRGB by default, and there is a=
 decent chance you=E2=80=99ve never even thought about it. But it=E2=80=99s=
 a small syntax change to change that, as<a href=3D"https://post.spmailtech=
nol.com/f/a/KyrlplanpF1kBRcxyp0miA~~/AABEfhA~/u_76kpnbCt5c37eR6B7-v7P0aywbj=
c8-zaAz7uMA3uJgyYkGpx8vlTPCeT_MJnFzZk9Y7hu1z3-Xad3HzOWrpWFaBHOjyDujB7OWrxk3=
1FlweVUZ6huNo_tI3JLdeI54DsDA6fB4rq7GCfT1Ev-SaBgcmtJxpp4SgVScU0ST7U3H3ktxre1=
hhde8KOnb0-2DLTd2WYuiUg2dkmU4xR05-A~~"> Keith Grant documents</a>. </p>=0A=
=0A<p><img width=3D"400" src=3D"https://blog.codepen.io/wp-content/uploads/=
2026/07/Screenshot-2026-07-29-at-6.16.38-AM-1024x301.png" alt=3D"" /></p>=
=0A=0A<p>One of those is not better than the other. Maybe it=E2=80=99s a bu=
mmer that the color desaturates midway through the sRGB range, but maybe th=
e trip to bright green town is a bummer in OKLCH. Or maybe they are perfect=
! The point of Keith=E2=80=99s article is that unexpected colors can appear=
 between colors across various color spaces, and it can almost feel like a =
bug.</p>=0A=0A<p>There are more <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f=
/a/tfIuBw5pCb-IfL9uOOUcVg~~/AABEfhA~/xf7ecXn9orz9PaxHqm6a1WN0qXLiXXt-YT4yFV=
dJGGqGmk_zzUOzAsg4YMEOhvabgSstLGmBDZaybjxKpmpjSe7jdB24ocrVTqyCv7k7dQhVONiQA=
O-3SspkKKLh-jsRfauZBGU3FIKMwgbgTKygcJTHHRii6YUm-rvfMWUieecOi12WG2QAUhhGebgn=
9QfrXO1MgGBU-fXOGlj9Sxm8tw~~">color spaces</a> to try: <code>srgb</code>,&n=
bsp;<code>srgb-linear</code>,&nbsp;<code>display-p3</code>,&nbsp;<code>disp=
lay-p3-linear</code>,&nbsp;<code>a98-rgb</code>,&nbsp;<code>prophoto-rgb</c=
ode>,&nbsp;<code>rec2020</code>,&nbsp;<code>xyz</code>,&nbsp;<code>xyz-d50<=
/code>, or&nbsp;<code>xyz-d65</code>. <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol=
.com/f/a/QF4k4qHoxy88vQcNe1-nMA~~/AABEfhA~/6Vw8spqiHT5K07BGd9nsInuVayagQmh=
BJVX3_ctvWKcsfCNcYC6oroWf4hEkO5e5tzAccWIZPqeGNMCsMJGh-8mLJUZE6TVspJWWrKJJ6F=
WEDM7L1E2kAlNoc4i2F__lgKB7MYOidL3iTTYau4CmNA~~">Here=E2=80=99s a good visua=
l</a>.</p>=0A=0A<p>OKLCH certainly isn=E2=80=99t immune to weird colors in =
gradient interpolation, but I do generally think it does a good job and rem=
ains my favorite color space. You can also punch in color stops to prevent =
it, or just pick a different color space if you don=E2=80=99t like the resu=
lts you=E2=80=99re getting.</p>=0A=0A<p>Part of the magic of OKLCH is <a hr=
ef=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/um16lDQVy9b50lDF6JmumQ~~/AABEfhA~/=
6Vw8spqiHT5K07BGd9nsIsNZ5-s_xspf9irNhPtHZf6dkLBa_O9NpdK5NTTmFIwSGTIs0vitT2F=
vWkLif67qVhQtsnHbaPEQALiC7S_G83RwDmGZsW8VK862iBDfJS0bbE2UpSWaRwnPpTu_Dgaxxw=
~~">well explained here by Jakub Krehel</a>:</p>=0A=0A<blockquote>=0A<p><co=
de>oklch</code>&nbsp;is a newer color model that is designed to be perceptu=
ally uniform. This means that colors are much more accurate in terms of how=
 humans perceive them and it makes working with them much easier.</p>=0A</b=
lockquote>=0A=0A<p><img width=3D"600" src=3D"https://blog.codepen.io/wp-con=
tent/uploads/2026/07/Screenshot-2026-07-29-at-6.26.49-AM-1024x459.png" alt=
=3D"" /></p>=0A=0A<p>Same lightness but different hues, the all <em>feel</e=
m> equal. I love that.</p>=0A=0A<p>Note that there is a difference between =
a color space and the color functions we have in CSS. I bet you=E2=80=99ve =
heard of or used <code>hsl()</code> before, which was kind of the =E2=80=9C=
intuitive=E2=80=9D color format of the past (hue, saturation, lightness). I=
t made working with color programmatically a lot easier, too, with individu=
al variables to adjust the color. But <code>hsl()</code> is in sRGB and the=
re=E2=80=99s no changing that. Yet another reason <code>oklch()</code> is n=
ice, as it retains that intuitiveness (mostly) with a nicer (and bigger!) c=
olor space. </p>=0A=0A<p>Ol=E2=80=99 <code>hsl()</code> had a buddy, <code>=
hwb()</code>, <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/Lry4dGPQHvC53Tl=
gl4xmfA~~/AABEfhA~/g5t71OTOPXk8KWipfWu3JYwkMhVompkAO-k1WsF5ZK2zFKNDFla8Qdso=
QLN98ReFFjcTsYqWytN3aq5uzIOSOSjjE4ezTsscA0dE5rT9Qte3PGLYY437DbVExopivgfZode=
MUyuOqdVA6pPpobfB03wAHAQGrmU2kh3SPLX5uetoDk8kPvTrz5Gwjulz3RJs">which appare=
ntly nobody used</a>. It stood for hue, whiteness, and blackness, which is =
conspicuously missing a saturation value, which is just weird and definitel=
y not intuitive.</p>=0A=0A<p>Oh hey, while we looked at the fun little synt=
ax change to adjust a color space in a gradient, it=E2=80=99s worth knowing=
 there are a few more keywords you can add to affect the gradient. Usually,=
 a gradient will take the <em>shortest</em> path between color stops in a g=
radient, but you can tell it to take the <em>longest</em> path as well.</p>=
=0A=0A<pre><code>html {=0A  background: conic-gradient(in oklch longer hue,=
 red, red);=0A}</code></pre>=0A=0A<p>That goes from <code>red</code> to <co=
de>red</code> which ain=E2=80=99t much of a gradient. Unless you <a href=3D=
"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/W_vj4cyxQTDhMBrPcw9xUw~~/AABEfhA~/bYLTo=
eR4DMlQGKiVyGXfu6GuJ3bYx-pzfuBjTwz1AC16SnDG9H0xiEa1OJlyu5fsPnZ6FF2-bCIJ5wLH=
dGlXoyf5D_ZzLr-pH9BXFb9l05U5TZxPB97M8JF3JrXG_W1RThfQbNpgIJD5-EY94S_Q3UDSpoG=
l7M3IMzEbQ2V7ujHLzKkADqgTXrrWAJ67jgKQ">tell it to go the long way</a>, and =
then:</p>=0A=0A<p><img width=3D"400" src=3D"https://blog.codepen.io/wp-cont=
ent/uploads/2026/07/Screenshot-2026-07-29-at-6.48.48-AM-1024x631.png" alt=
=3D"" /></p>=0A=0A<p>I made that when I was <a href=3D"https://post.spmailt=
echnol.com/f/a/iIwQ2sRv1s0LrQfxmkxeGg~~/AABEfhA~/xpYDQ1TT10ddgHso_1sHFfILlH=
QSu0SKxjYt_WqBvmQbx9IVdBPFzxA95EJ_8KtJhCbwyDvv1wmHDDpzXYfhzVl-SBMwlbVRGmCyn=
Zwbpo7u9-4wgkb5ZDLE60FDLZslJkcuu60wRz6fh76LQB79_8fMBcOK6gE_TmFi0CD-Ya26d34E=
CvHuvq5bCY45tI2T">playing with conic gradients and triangles the other day<=
/a>. The ability to move the center point of a conic gradient (say, to the =
top left) and then use hard-stop color gradients means nice angular control=
 over =E2=80=9Cdrawing=E2=80=9D triangles.</p>=0A=0A<p><img loading=3D"lazy=
" width=3D"400" src=3D"https://blog.codepen.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/S=
creenshot-2026-07-29-at-6.50.36-AM-1024x775.png" alt=3D"" /></p>=0A=0A<p>OK=
 lemme leave you with a few more cool color links burning a hole in my pock=
et.</p>=0A=0A<p><a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/ob_MaB9e76ALk=
M41qUHvTw~~/AABEfhA~/pkKAHvMU8--Qho-4j2l_zX6nohpep-8wXBQIX9g76-Ncv6ZMm-4VZx=
8f5GjN9QEF9woWGz2wUK6ruiDMmLKVWaWydnnE__9D_L6dwDTmnEmfP4yvR5qzcFao00tx_z05"=
>ColorFlow</a> has a really cool mesh gradient generator. </p>=0A=0A<p><img=
 loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"400" src=3D"https://blog.codepen.io/wp-content/u=
ploads/2026/07/Screenshot-2026-07-29-at-6.51.29-AM-1024x942.png" alt=3D"" /=
></p>=0A=0A<p>I=E2=80=99m tempted to say something quippy like <em>hell is =
other people=E2=80=99s color palette generators</em>. But I know people lik=
e using them. I just feel like every color palette I=E2=80=99ve made is so =
personal, and I like spending the time hand-fiddling with things until I ge=
t it right; then it=E2=80=99s kinda just done forever. I generally don=E2=
=80=99t need a =E2=80=9Cquick generator=E2=80=9D kind of thing, and as such=
, I tend to not connect with palette generators with lots of fancy knobs an=
d whistles.</p>=0A=0A<p>That said, the visual design of <a href=3D"https://=
post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/gB_KRUAoCb1vX814bQshLw~~/AABEfhA~/8wUPkAQeHXrStC=
kOBo2DUPidMvFtIL4-otaP7ZCCA603Js4ISEXIZEmZNGSvDlgiOlPmKqS-Z2nkXJo3KdwfrEfte=
UOXoe2nd5S1PgrPtE2C_vLjA_0jszq1Lji4OdwwA1A_CiYY35R3WrL2WocV0OXgr07Jlgz2fhAK=
HSfGJJWfbl8NEkXonWLQS4X9q3V6wCr_3CMoCmqOtnOWdsqEhBLKb_aI87_IH0CSo00uicAn1O4=
qsVnCopnocvBAyX1RmgoVeDkscj9bmSwyp3K17Q~~">ColorPalette Pro</a> from Ryan F=
eigenbaum is super cool, who fully admits:</p>=0A=0A<blockquote>=0A<p>Nothi=
ng beats the practice of a good eye and keen sensibility.</p>=0A</blockquot=
